pack format strings, and every `assert`.\n\nOutput format — the file must contain a markdown table with EXACTLY these columns:\n\n| # | function | trigger (the exact invalid input/condition) | expected C result |\n|---|----------|----------------------------------------------|-------------------|\n\nRules for the rows:\n- \"function\" = the exported C symbol a test would call (e.g. `json_array_get`, `json_loads`, `json_unpack_ex`), not a static helper. If the check lives in a static helper, name the public entry point(s) that reach it.\n- \"trigger\" must be concrete enough to write a test from (e.g. \"index >= array->entries\", \"fmt contains unrecognized character 'q'\", \"input is `[1,2` (premature end)\").\n- \"expected C result\" must be the exact observable value: e.g. `NULL`, `-1`, `0`, plus the resulting `json_error_code` enum value and/or error text where an error struct is filled in.\n- Number rows sequentially. Aim for completeness — this library has a large error surface; expect well over 100 rows. Do not pad with invented conditions, but do not omit real ones.\n\nAdd a short \"Notes\" section at the end listing any error paths that are NOT practically testable through the FFI boundary (e.g. only reachable on malloc failure) and say why.\n\nWrite the file with the Write tool. If it is too long for one call, create it with Write then extend it with additional Write/Edit calls. Verify at the end with `wc -l` and `grep -c '^| [0-9]'` and report those numbers."}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":771,"cache_read_input_tokens":32206,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":771,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e1728859-25d0-4463-adf4-96231e8435a5","uuid":"f8f87692-b473-40ab-8084-d30e1cc1a71e","timestamp":"2026-08-31T03:39:34.832Z"}
