{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_36fdq4btl63udwlf3bwtfee57ozszmad4c26x35rdk7wq4zglirq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01RXYbdK9gmBjZBhDsUKjGCo","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-6oQ0Bh/translation/CONFIGS_part3.md","content":"# CONFIGS part 3 — low-level support modules (hashtable.c, hashtable_seed.c, strconv.c, dtoa.c entry points)\n\nConstants derived from the tree: `INITIAL_HASHTABLE_ORDER = 3` (jansson_private_config.h:36) → 8 buckets; `hashsize(n) = 1<<n`, `hashmask(n) = hashsize(n)-1` (lookup3.h:76-77). Grow condition (hashtable.c:234): at the **top** of `hashtable_set`, `if (size >= hashsize(order)) hashtable_do_rehash()` → order+1, i.e. rehash fires on the 9th, 17th, 33rd, 65th … `hashtable_set` call, *before* the key is even looked up. Bucket index is `hash & hashmask(order)` in set/get/del/iter_at but `hash % new_size` in `hashtable_do_rehash` (hashtable.c:167). `insert_to_bucket` **prepends** (bucket->first = list), so `hashtable->list` order is reverse-insertion within a bucket, while `ordered_list` is appended at the tail → **iteration order is pure insertion order and is seed-independent**. Config also sets `DTOA_ENABLED=1`, `USE_URANDOM=1`, `HAVE_ATOMIC_BUILTINS=1`, `HAVE_SCHED_YIELD=1`, `HAVE_OPEN/CLOSE/READ=1`, `HAVE_GETTIMEOFDAY=1`, `HAVE_GETPID=1` → the `__atomic_*` `json_object_seed` and the `dtoa_r`-based `jsonp_dtostr` are the live variants.\n\n| # | entry point(s) | configuration (options set + input shape) | [ ] |\n|---|----------------|--------------------------------------------|-----|\n| 1 | `hashtable_init` + `hashtable_close` | fresh `hashtable_t`; assert return 0, `size==0`, `order==3`, all 8 buckets have `first==last==&ht->list`, `list`/`ordered_list` self-linked; immediately `hashtable_close` (empty `hashtable_do_clear` loop, frees buckets) | [ ] |\n| 2 | `hashtable_init` + `hashtable_get` + `hashtable_iter` | empty table, no inserts: `hashtable_get(ht,\"x\",1)` → NULL (`bucket_is_empty` early return in `hashtable_find_pair`), `hashtable_iter(ht)` → NULL (`list->next == &ordered_list`) | [ ] |\n| 3 | `hashtable_set` | 1 insert, key `\"a\"` len 1, value `json_integer(1)`; size 0 < 8 so **no** rehash; bucket was empty → `list_insert(&ht->list, ...)`, `bucket->first==bucket->last==&pair->list`; assert size 1, `hashtable_get` returns the same pointer | [ ] |\n| 4 | `hashtable_set` ×7 | insert 7 distinct keys `\"k0\"..\"k6\"` (below the grow threshold: size reaches 7 < 8, never rehashes); assert `order==3` throughout, size 7, every key retrievable, iteration yields insertion order `k0..k6` | [ ] |\n| 5 | `hashtable_set` ×8 | insert 8 distinct keys `\"k0\"..\"k7\"` — the 8th call sees size 7 < 8 so still no rehash; land exactly on the threshold: after the loop `size==8 == hashsize(3)` with `order` still 3 (load factor exactly 1) | [ ] |\n| 6 | `hashtable_set` ×9 | insert 9 distinct keys — the 9th call sees `size==8 >= hashsize(3)` → **first rehash** to `order==4` / 16 buckets, then inserts; assert `order==4`, size 9, all 9 keys still retrievable, iteration order unchanged (`k0..k8`) | [ ] |\n| 7 | `hashtable_set` ×17 then ×33 then ×65 | cross the threshold repeatedly: assert `order` transitions 3→4 at insert #9, 4→5 at #17, 5→6 at #33, 6→7 at #65; after each rehash re-`hashtable_get` every previously inserted key and re-walk the iterator to confirm insertion order is preserved across all four rehashes | [ ] |\n| 8 | `hashtable_set` (existing key at threshold) | insert 8 distinct keys so `size==8`, then `hashtable_set` with an **already-present** key: the rehash check runs first → table grows to `order==4` even though no new pair is added; then `json_decref(old)` + `pair->value = new`, size stays 8 | [ ] |\n| 9 | `hashtable_set` (replace value) | table with keys `\"a\",\"b\",\"c\"`; `hashtable_set(ht,\"b\",1,json_string(\"new\"))` → `hashtable_find_pair` hits, old 