1 object is a use-after-free trap worth pinning) | [ ] |\n| 32 | `hashtable_*` randomized model check | random op sequence (≥5000 ops) over a keyspace of ~200 keys drawn from {ASCII, empty, embedded-NUL, 0..255 binary, 1 KiB long} with ops weighted set 50% / get 25% / del 15% / iter-walk 5% / clear 5%; compare against a reference ordered map, and after every op assert `size`, `order`, full iteration sequence, and per-key get results match the C implementation byte-for-byte | [ ] |\n| 33 | `hashtable_seed` (data symbol) | `extern volatile uint32_t hashtable_seed` starts at **0** (hashtable_seed.c:194); assert it is 0 before any `json_object_seed`/object creation, and that `hash_str` with seed 0 is a well-defined (if unseeded) hash — i.e. a table built before seeding must still behave consistently | [ ] |\n| 34 | `json_object_seed(0)` | seed 0 → `generate_seed()` path: with `USE_URANDOM` + `HAVE_OPEN/CLOSE/READ`, `seed_from_urandom` does `open(\"/dev/urandom\",O_RDONLY)` + `read` of 4 bytes + `buf_to_uint32` (big-endian assembly of `data[0..3]`); assert `hashtable_seed != 0` afterwards and that two separate processes get different values | [ ] |\n| 35 | `json_object_seed(fixed)` | `json_object_seed(0xDEADBEEF)` on a fresh process → `hashtable_seed == 0xDEADBEEF` exactly (`new_seed = (uint32_t)seed`, no generation); also `json_object_seed((size_t)0x1_0000_0001)` on 64-bit → truncated to `0x00000001` | [ ] |\n| 36 | `json_object_seed` twice | call `json_object_seed(0xAAAA)` then `json_object_seed(0xBBBB)`: the second call sees `hashtable_seed != 0` and is a **no-op** (outer `if`), seed stays `0xAAAA`; also `json_object_seed(0)` then `json_object_seed(0x1234)` → stays the random value | [ ] |\n| 37 | `json_object_seed` (atomic-builtins variant, the live one) | `HAVE_ATOMIC_BUILTINS && (HAVE_SCHED_YIELD || !_WIN32)` → first caller wins `__atomic_test_and_set(&seed_initialized, RELAXED)==0` and does `__atomic_store_n(&hashtable_seed, new_seed, RELEASE)`; second concurrent 