eftright=0`, trailing zeros suppressed by dtoa. Assert output digits count <= prec and exact string match against the C reference. | [ ] |\n| 47 | `jsonp_dtostr` with `prec` at/above the internal `digits[25]` limit | `precision` = 18, 23, 24 (ok: `blen=25 > i=24`), then 25 and 26 and 100. For `precision >= 25`, `dtoa_r`'s `blen <= (size_t)i` test fires → returns NULL → `jsonp_dtostr` returns **-1** with `buffer` untouched. Assert the exact cutoff is at 25. | [ ] |\n| 48 | `jsonp_dtostr` with negative `prec` | `precision` = -1, -17, `INT_MIN`. `precision != 0` → `mode = 2`; then `ndigits <= 0` → `ndigits = 1`, so output is 1 significant digit. Assert e.g. `0.1, prec=-1` → `\"0.1\"`, `123456.0, prec=-1` → `\"100000.0\"`-shaped result matching the reference exactly. | [ ] |\n| 49 | `jsonp_dtostr` exponent-form branch (`decpt <= -4`) | Values whose `decpt <= -4`: `1e-5` (decpt=-4), `1e-4` (decpt=-3 → NO exponent, prints `0.0001`), `9.9e-5`, `5e-324`, `1e-308`, `-1e-308`. Assert the `decpt<=-4` boundary is exact: `0.0001` → fixed notation, `0.00001` → `1e-05`-style (`e` + `sprintf(\"%d\", exp)` → `\"1e-05\"` vs `\"1e-5\"`: the reference uses `%d` on `exp=decpt-1`, so `1e-5` prints as `1e-05`? — assert against the C reference byte-for-byte, do not assume). | [ ] |\n| 50 | `jsonp_dtostr` exponent-form branch (`decpt > 16`) | Values with `decpt == 16` (e.g. `1234567890123456.0`) → fixed notation with `\".0\"` appended; `decpt == 17` (e.g. `1e16`) → `use_exp=1`, `exp = decpt-1 = 16`, `decpt=1`. Also `1e100` (exp=100, 3 exponent digits — the \"max 3 numerical digits\" size budget), `1e308` (exp=308), `-1e308` (sign + 3 digits, the widest positive-exponent case). | [ ] |\n| 51 | `jsonp_dtostr` integral-value \".0\" padding | `1.0`, `-1.0`, `100.0`, `1e15`, `1e16-2`, `123456789012345.0`, `2.0`, `0.0`. These take `digits_len < decpt` → right-zero padding then `*p++='.'` then `memset(p,'0', vdigits_end-decpt)` with `vdigits_end == decpt+1` (from `!use_exp` branch) → exactly one t