backs up so only `\"inf\"` is consumed; assert `*end`), `\"nan\"`, `\"NAN\"`, `\"NaN\"`, `\"-nan\"`, `\"nan()\"`, `\"nan(0x1234)\"` and `\"nan(1234)\"` (the `hexnan` path), `\"nan(bad\"` (unterminated). Assert `isinf`/`isnan`, the sign bit, the NaN payload words where applicable, and `*end`. Note `bc.dplen` must be 0 for this path — so `\".inf\"` must NOT parse as infinity. | [ ] |\n| 88 | `strtod__unused` hex-float delegation | `\"0x1p0\"`, `\"0X1P0\"`, `\"0x1.8p3\"`, `\"-0x1p-1\"`, `\"0x0\"`, `\"0xg\"`, `\"0x1p99999999999\"`, `\"0x1p-99999999999\"`. Confirms the `if (*s=='0') switch(s[1]){case 'x': case 'X': gethex(&s,&rv,1,sign); goto ret;}` dispatch with `rounding` hard-wired to 1 (Round_near) in non-`Honor_FLT_ROUNDS` builds, and that the sign is applied after `ret` (`return sign ? -dval(&rv) : dval(&rv)`). Assert bits, `*end`, `errno`. | [ ] |\n| 89 | `strtod__unused` overflow / underflow | Overflow: `\"1e309\"`, `\"1e400\"`, `\"-1e400\"`, `\"1.8e308\"`, `\"1e19999\"`, a 400-digit integer → `±inf` (or DBL_MAX under directed rounding) with `errno == ERANGE`. Underflow: `\"1e-400\"`, `\"-1e-400\"`, `\"1e-19999\"`, `\"4.9406564584124654e-324\"` (exact subnormal, no ERANGE), `\"2.4703282292062327e-324\"` (rounds to 0 or to the smallest subnormal, ERANGE). Assert bits, sign of zero, and `errno` for each; clear `errno` before every call. | [ ] |\n| 90 | `strtod__unused` hard round-trip / bigcomp cases | Inputs known to force the slow paths: `\"9007199254740993\"` (2^53+1), `\"1e23\"`, `\"8.98846567431158e307\"`, `\"2.2250738585072011e-308\"` (the classic PHP hang case), `\"0.500000000000000166533453693773481063544750213623046875\"`, `\"1.0000000000000000000000000000000000000000001\"`, and 20 randomly generated 30-digit decimal strings with exponents in [-320, 320]. Assert bit-exact agreement with the reference (this is the differential core for `strtod__unused`). | [ ] |\n| 91 | `jsonp_malloc` | Default allocators. `jsonp_malloc(0)` → **NULL** without calling `do_malloc` (verify with a counting hook). `jsonp_malloc(1)`, `(16)`, `(4096)`, `(1<<20)` → non-NULL, writable for the full size. `jsonp_malloc(SIZE_MAX)` → NULL (real malloc failure, no crash). | [ ] |\n| 92 | `jsonp_free` | `jsonp_free(NULL)` → early return, `do_free` not called (counting hook). `jsonp_free(p)` for a `jsonp_malloc`'d pointer → `do_free` called exactly once with that pointer. Also free a pointer obtained through a custom malloc while a custom free is installed. | [ ] |\n| 93 | `jsonp_realloc` with the default (non-NULL) realloc slot | Default state → `do_realloc == realloc`, so `jsonp_realloc(ptr, originalSize, newSize)` calls `realloc(ptr, newSize)` and **ignores `originalSize`** (pass a deliberately wrong `originalSize`, e.g. 0 or `SIZE_MAX`, and assert the data is still preserved — proving `originalSize` is unused on this path). Cases: grow 16→32, shrink 32→16, `ptr == NULL` (acts as malloc), `newSize == 0` (implementation-defined: record what the reference returns), `newSize == SIZE_MAX` (failure → NULL, original pointer still valid). | [ ] |\n| 94 | `jsonp_realloc` emulation path (`do_realloc == NULL`) | `json_set_alloc_funcs(counting_malloc, counting_free)` → nulls the realloc slot. Then: (a) `jsonp_realloc(ptr, 16, 32)` → malloc(32) + `memcpy(new, ptr, min(16,32)==16)` + free(ptr); assert only the first 16 bytes are defined and the counters show 1 malloc + 1 free. (b) `jsonp_realloc(ptr, 32, 16)` → copies `min(32,16)==16` bytes (truncation). (c) `jsonp_realloc(ptr, 16, 16)`. (d) `jsonp_realloc(NULL, 0, 32)` → malloc only, no free, no memcpy. | [ ] |\n| 95 | `jsonp_realloc` emulation path with `newSize == 0` | With `do_realloc == NULL`: (a) `jsonp_realloc(ptr, 16, 0)` with `ptr != NULL` → `do_free(ptr)` is called and **NULL** is returned; (b) `jsonp_realloc(NULL, 0, 0)` → NULL with no free call. Assert the free counter in both. | [ ] |\n| 96 | `jsonp_realloc` emulation path with a failing malloc | `do_realloc == NULL` and a malloc that returns NULL: `jsonp_realloc(ptr, 16, 32)` → returns NULL and, per the `if ((newMemory != NULL) && (ptr != NULL))` guard, the **original `ptr` is NOT freed**. Assert the free counter is 0 and that `ptr` is still readable/freeable. | [ ] |\n| 97 | `json_set_alloc_funcs` / `json_get_alloc_funcs` | `json_set_alloc_funcs(m, f)` then `json_get_alloc_funcs(&om, &of)` → `om==m`, `of==f`. Then `json_get_alloc_funcs2(&om, &orr, &of)` → `orr == NULL` (the 2-arg setter nulls the realloc slot). Also call `json_get_alloc_funcs(NULL, NULL)`, `(&om, NULL)`, `(NULL, &of)` and assert no writes to the omitted slots (canary the locals). | [ ] |\n| 98 | `json_set_alloc_funcs2` / `json_get_alloc_funcs2` | `json_set_alloc_funcs2(m, r, f)` then `json_get_alloc_funcs2(&om,&orr,&of)` → all three match; `json_get_alloc_funcs(&om,&of)` → matches m/f. Then `json_set_alloc_funcs2(m, NULL, f)` (explicitly NULL realloc) → getter reports NULL and `jsonp_realloc` takes the emulation path. NULL out-params for `json_get_alloc_funcs2` in all 8 combinations. | [ ] |\n| 99 | setter ordering interaction: 2-arg after 3-arg | `json_set_alloc_funcs2(m1, r1, f1)`; assert `jsonp_realloc` calls `r1`. Then `json_set_alloc_funcs(m2, f2)`; assert `do_realloc` is now **NULL** (getter2 reports NULL) and `jsonp_realloc` switches to the malloc+memcpy+free emulation using `m2`/`f2` — `r1` must never be called again. Exercise through a real `strbuffer_append_bytes` growth to confirm the behavioural difference. | [ ] |\n| 100 | setter ordering interaction: 3-arg after 2-arg | `json_set_alloc_funcs(m1, f1)` (realloc slot NULL, emulation active); then `json_set_alloc_funcs2(m2, r2, f2)`; assert `jsonp_realloc` now calls `r2` directly and `originalSize` becomes irrelevant again. Finish by restoring defaults with `json_set_alloc_funcs2(malloc, realloc, free)` and assert `jsonp_realloc` preserves data across a grow. | [ ] |\n| 101 | `jsonp_strndup` | `jsonp_strndup(\"hello\", len)` for `len` = 0 (→ `\"\"`, 1 byte allocated), 1, 4, 5 (==