/with/a/long/name/input.json\"` (79 chars), the same at exactly 80 and at 120 chars, a Windows-style `\"C:\\\\dir\\\\...\\\\file.json\"`, a source that is *only* separators, and a source whose 80-char truncation point lands mid-path-component. This code performs **no** separator-aware trimming — assert the plain `\"...\"`-prefix truncation, not a path-aware one. | [ ] |\n| 106 | `jsonp_error_set_source` NULL guards | `jsonp_error_set_source(NULL, \"x\")`, `jsonp_error_set_source(&err, NULL)`, and `jsonp_error_set_source(NULL, NULL)` → all early-return with `&err` completely unmodified (canary the whole struct). | [ ] |\n| 107 | `jsonp_error_set` / `jsonp_error_vset` basic store | Freshly `jsonp_error_init`'d error. `jsonp_error_set(&err, 3, 7, 42, json_error_invalid_syntax, \"bad token '%c' at %d\", 'x', 7)`. Assert `line==3`, `column==7`, `position==42`, `text` == the formatted message, `text[JSON_ERROR_TEXT_LENGTH-1] == json_error_invalid_syntax`, and `json_error_code(&err)` reads it back. Also negative `line`/`column` (-1) and `position == 0`. | [ ] |\n| 108 | `jsonp_error_vset` \"first error wins\" | Call `jsonp_error_set` twice with different line/column/position/code/message. The second call sees `error->text[0] != '\\0'` and returns immediately → all fields keep the first call's values. Edge case: a first call whose formatted message is **empty** (`msg == \"\"`) leaves `text[0]=='\\0'`, so a second call **does** overwrite — assert that asymmetry. Also `jsonp_error_set(NULL, ...)` → no-op. | [ ] |\n| 109 | `jsonp_error_vset` message-length truncation | Messages of formatted length 0, 1, 100, **157**, **158**, **159**, 160, 300, 1000 (e.g. `\"%s\"` with a generated string). `vsnprintf(text, 159, ...)` writes at most 158 chars + NUL, then `text[158] = '\\0'` and `text[159] = code`. Assert: for length <= 157 the text is intact; at 158 the last char is dropped by the explicit `text[158]='\\0'`; for longer inputs the text is exactly the first 158 characters; `text[159]` always holds the code and is never clobbered. Also a message ending mid-multibyte-UTF-8 sequence at the 158-byte cut. | [ ] |\n| 110 | `jsonp_error_vset` every `json_error_code` value | Loop `code` over all 18 enumerators (`json_error_unknown`=0 … `json_error_index_out_of_range`=17) plus out-of-enum values 18, 127, 128, 255 and -1 (cast). For each, `jsonp_error_set(&err, 1, 1, 1, code, \"m\")` on a freshly init'd error and assert `error->text[159] == (char)code` and `json_error_code(&err) == code`. Note `char` may be signed: assert what the reference produces for 128/255/-1. | [ ] |\n| 111 | `jsonp_error_set` varargs / format coverage | Format strings exercising the varargs plumbing: `\"%s\"` (short and 300-char argument), `\"%d %d %d\"`, `\"%c\"` with `'\\0'` and with a byte >= 0x80, `\"%.*s\"` with a precision argument, `\"%zu\"` with a `size_t`, `\"%%\"`, `\"%p\"`, a format with 8+ arguments, and a format containing a literal newline and embedded quotes. Also call `jsonp_error_vset` directly with a `va_list` (and re-use the same `va_list` via `va_copy` for a second call to confirm the first-error-wins guard). Also `position` values `0`, `INT_MAX`, `(size_t)INT_MAX+1`, `SIZE_MAX` to exercise the `(int)position` truncation. | [ ] |\n| 112 | `utf8_encode` 1-byte range | `codepoint` = 0, 1, 0x41, 0x7E, **0x7F** (last 1-byte). Assert return 0, `*size == 1`, `buffer[0] == codepoint`. Also codepoint 0 must write a real NUL byte and report size 1. | [ ] |\n| 113 | `utf8_encode` 2-, 3-, 4-byte ranges and boundaries | `0x80` (first 2-byte) → `C2 80`; `0x7FF` (last 2-byte) → `DF BF`; `0x800` (first 3-byte) → `E0 A0 80`; `0xFFFF` → `EF BF BF`; `0x10000` (first 4-byte) → `F0 90 80 80`; `0x10FFFF` (last valid) → `F4 8F BF BF`. Assert `*size` is 2/2/3/3/4/4 and the exact bytes (note the code uses `0xC0 + ...` on a `char` buffer, so the stored bytes may be negative `char`s — compare as `unsigned char`). | [ ] |\n| 114 | `utf8_encode` rejections and the surrogate hole | `co