{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01Rgxm2DN7gm77cs6gwtYyPF","type":"tool_result","content":"434 CONFIGS.md\n# CONFIGS.md — CONFIGURATION-SURFACE TABLE (valid inputs)\n\nEvery row is a configuration the C code actually distinguishes, derived\nmechanically from the branches in the C source. Phase B requires each row to\npass a differential test across many randomized inputs before it is checked.\n\nRow numbering is global and continuous across sections.\n\n## A. Encoding — dump.c\n\n| # | entry point(s) | configuration (options set + input shape) | [ ] |\n|---|----------------|--------------------------------------------|-----|\n| 1 | `json_dumps` | `flags = 0` (no indent, no compact). Input `[1,2,3]`. Exercises `dump_indent` with `FLAGS_TO_INDENT==0` and `space==0` at open/close (emits nothing) and `space==1` after each comma with `!(flags&JSON_COMPACT)` (emits one `\" \"`). Expect `[1, 2, 3]`. | [ ] |\n| 2 | `json_dumps` | `flags = JSON_INDENT(0)` — numerically identical to `flags=0`; assert byte-identical output to row 1 for the same input `[1,2,3]`. Confirms `JSON_INDENT(0)` sets no bits. | [ ] |\n| 3 | `json_dumps` | `flags = JSON_INDENT(1)`. Input `[1,[2,3],{\"a\":4}]`. `ws_count=1`, `n_spaces = depth*1`; every `dump_indent` call takes the `>0` branch: `\"\\n\"` then `depth` spaces. Checks depth accounting at depth 1 and 2. | [ ] |\n| 4 | `json_dumps` | `flags = JSON_INDENT(2)`. Input `{\"a\":[1,2],\"b\":{\"c\":null}}`. Verifies `depth+1` indent after `{`/`[`, `depth+1` after commas (`space=1` ignored because indent branch is taken first), and `depth` indent before the closing brace/bracket. | [ ] |\n| 5 | `json_dumps` | `flags = JSON_INDENT(4)`. Input nested 4 levels: `[[[[1]]]]`. Deepest `dump_indent` gets `n_spaces = 4*4 = 16` (< 32, single `dump(whitespace,16)` call). | [ ] |\n| 6 | `json_dumps` | `flags = JSON_INDENT(31)` (== `JSON_MAX_INDENT`, 0x1F). Input `[1]` — `n_spaces = 1*31 = 31`, exactly `sizeof whitespace - 1`; one chunk, no loop repeat. Boundary of the 32-space `whitespace[]` literal. | [ ] |\n| 7 | `json_dumps` | `flags = JSON_INDENT(31)`. Input `[[1]]` — inner elements at depth 2 give `n_spaces = 62 > 31`, forcing the `while (n_spaces > 0)` chunking loop to run twice (31 + 31). Closing indent at depth 1 is 31. | [ ] |\n| 8 | `json_dumps` | `flags = JSON_INDENT(31)`. Input `[[[1]]]` — depth 3 → `n_spaces = 93`, chunk loop runs 3 times (31+31+31, last chunk exact). Verifies `cur_n = n_spaces < 31 ? n_spaces : 31` when the remainder is 0 vs partial. | [ ] |\n| 9 | `json_dumps` | `flags = JSON_INDENT(5)`. Input `[[[1]]]` — depth 3 → `n_spaces = 15`; also depth 7 variant (`[[[[[[[1]]]]]]]` → 35) to hit a *partial* final chunk (31 + 4). | [ ] |\n| 10 | `json_dumps` | Indent × empty containers, `flags = JSON_INDENT(2)`. Inputs `[]`, `{}`, `[[],{}]`, `{\"a\":[],\"b\":{}}`. Empty array returns via the `n == 0` early return and empty object via the `!iter` early return — **before** any `dump_indent`, so no newline/spaces appear inside `[]`/`{}`. Expect `[]`, `{}` with no interior whitespace at any indent. | [ ] |\n| 11 | `json_dumps` | `flags = JSON_COMPACT` alone. Input `{\"a\":1,\"b\":[2,3]}`. `separator = \":\"`, `separator_length = 1`; `dump_indent` with indent 0 and `space=1` hits the `space && !(flags&JSON_COMPACT)` test which is false → no space after commas. Expect `{\"a\":1,\"b\":[2,3]}`. | [ ] |\n| 12 | `json_dumps` | `flags = 0` on the same object as row 11 to pin the non-compact `separator = \": \"`, `separator_length = 2`. Expect `{\"a\": 1, \"b\": [2, 3]}`. | [ ] |\n| 13 | `json_dumps` | `flags = JSON_COMPACT \\| JSON_INDENT(0)`. Identical bits to row 11; assert byte-identical output. | [ ] |\n| 14 | `json_dumps` | `flags = JSON_COMPACT \\| JSON_INDENT(1)`. Input `{\"a\":1,\"b\":2}`. Indent branch wins in `dump_indent` (newline + spaces still emitted) but the object key separator stays `\":\"` with no trailing space. This is the interaction that COMPACT does *not* suppress. | [ ] |\n| 15 | `json_dumps` | `flags = JSON_COMPACT \\| JSON_INDENT(2)` and `... \\| JSON_INDENT(4)` on nested `{\"a\":[1,{\"b\":2}]}`. Same as row 14 at more depths; cross-check each indent value against non-compact output to isolate the `\": \"` → `\":\"` difference only. | [ ] |\n| 16 | `json_dumps` | `flags = JSON_COMPACT \\| JSON_INDENT(31)`. Input `[1,2]` — compact + max indent: newline + 31 spaces between elements, no space after comma from the `space` branch (unreachable), 31-space chunk boundary. | [ ] |\n| 17 | `json_dumps` | `flags = JSON_ENSURE_ASCII \\| JSON_ENCODE_ANY`. Input a bare string of pure ASCII printables `\"abc XYZ 019 !#$%&'()*+,-.:;<=>?@[]^_`{\\|}~\"`. All codepoints `<= 0x7F`, none mandatory-escaped, slash flag off → the inner `while` never breaks; single `dump(str, pos-str)` of the whole run then closing quote. | [ ] |\n| 18 | `json_dumps` | `flags = JSON_ENSURE_ASCII \\| JSON_ENCODE_ANY`. Input string with 2-byte UTF-8 (`\"é\"` U+00E9, `\"ÿ\"` U+00FF, `\"Ā\"` U+0100, `\"߿\"` U+07FF). Each breaks on `codepoint > 0x7F`, falls to `default:` with `codepoint < 0x10000` → `snprintf(\"\\\\u%04X\")`, `length = 6`. Expect `é` etc. — uppercase hex, 4 digits. | [ ] |","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_019aLJXHkzM6g67cTn5RRNt3","session_id":"e1728859-25d0-4463-adf4-96231e8435a5","uuid":"c6552503-3f66-43fe-85f8-5f97415635a8","timestamp":"2026-08-31T03:55:55.183Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Phase B tests for load.c"}
