ORT_KEYS|JSON_ENCODE_ANY)` compared byte-for-byte with `cbytes(...)`.\n- For `json_unpack`/`json_unpack_ex`: build the SAME root in both libraries (parse identical text with each library's `json_loads`), then unpack with identical formats and out-pointers. Compare the return value, the full error byte image, AND every value written through the out-pointers (ints, doubles as BITS, strings as raw bytes via `cbytes`, string lengths, booleans, and for `o`/`O` the type and a canonical dump of the extracted value). Pass POISONED out-pointer targets and assert that both libraries write exactly the same set of them — `JSON_VALIDATE_ONLY` must consume key varargs but leave value targets untouched, and that asymmetry is a prime source of porting bugs.\n- Cover `JSON_VALIDATE_ONLY` and `JSON_STRICT` alone AND together, with and without optional (`?`) keys, and note from the C that a trailing `*` sets `strict = -1` which SUPPRESSES the `JSON_STRICT` promotion — test that explicitly.\n- Test the `v*` entry points `json_vpack_ex` and `json_vunpack_ex` through the `vashim()` trampolines described in HARNESS.md, with a multi-argument format (e.g. `\"{s:s%,s:[i,I,f,b,n],s:O}\"`), on both the success path and an early-error path (the C does `va_copy`, so the error path is where vararg handling bugs hide).\n- Test `json_sprintf` and `json_vsprintf` (the latter via `vashim()`): a range of printf formats (`%s`, `%d`, `%i`, `%f`, `%g`, `%e`, `%x`, `%%`, width/precision/flags like `%5.2f` and `%-10s`, and a format producing a very long string), plus a format producing invalid UTF-8, and the empty format.\n- Cover nested formats several levels deep and randomized format/argument pairs generated together so the varargs always match the format.\n- Free every value with `decref(api, j)` and every dumped buffer with `jfree(api, ptr)`.\n\nSTEP 3 — run it: `cd translation && timeout 600 cargo test --release --test a08_pack_unpack 2>&1 | tail -40`\nIterate until it passes. `--release` is required