\n- **Flags:** `json_indent(n)` for all n 0..=31 (incl. 0,1,2,4,5,31), `JSON_COMPACT`, `JSON_ENSURE_ASCII`, `JSON_SORT_KEYS`, `JSON_PRESERVE_ORDER`, `JSON_ENCODE_ANY`, `JSON_ESCAPE_SLASH`, `json_real_precision(n)` for n 0..=31, `JSON_EMBED`, single-bit liveness map for bits 0..24, `0x800`, `0x20000`, `SIZE_MAX`, and random 64-bit flag words.\n- **Row 66 sweep:** nested indent×compact×ascii×sort×slash loops = 96 configurations, on one fixed mixed document plus **200 random documents** (2.6 MB of output compared byte-for-byte; largest document ≈3 KB). A guard assertion fails if the generator ever degenerates into trivial documents.\n- **Randomised builder** `build`/`build_container` drives all 8 json types, empty/1/2/few/many containers, nesting, `rng.json_int()`, `rng.real()`, `rng.ascii_string()`, `rng.utf8_string()`, embedded-NUL strings and keys, empty keys, and byte-wise-sorting key sets. Each library builds from the same seed, so the trees are structurally identical. Every test has its own fixed seed constant; every buffer is freed with `jfree(api, …)` and every value with `decref(api, …)`.\n\n### Two CONFIGS row claims that the C does *not* actually support (tests assert the real C behaviour instead of the row's guess)\n- **Row 75** claims `json_dumpb` can do \"non-prefix writes\". It cannot: `dump_to_buffer` increments `buf->used` even for a skipped chunk, so after the first skip `used > size` holds forever and every later chunk is skipped too. The test now proves the buffer always holds a plain truncated prefix that ends exactly on a chunk boundary, and that nothing is written past it.\n- **Row 88** asks for \"an array containing itself\" / \"an object containing itself\". `json_array_append_new` and `json_object_setn_new_nocheck` both refuse `json == value`, so the shortest constructible cycle is length 2. The test asserts the refusal (`-1`) and then builds 2-cycles and a 3-cycle, all of which `jsonp_loop_check` rejects, plus a shared-child DAG that must still succeed.\n\n### Also documented by assertions\n- Row 87's note: a callback failure that lands on an object **key** string is silently tolerated (`dump_string`'s return is ignored) — `dump_string` still aborts mid-key, but the overall dump reports success. The test asserts this happens and that all other failure sites stop the dump exactly at the failing chunk.\n- Row 86: the last chunk's return value is passed back verbatim (7 stays 7), not normalised to −1.\n- Row 78: dump.c never emits a zero-length chunk (which `fwrite(_,0,1,_)` would misreport as failure).\n- Row 67 liveness: bits 0–7 and 10–16 are live, bits 8 (`JSON_PRESERVE_ORDER`), 9 (`JSON_ENCODE_ANY`, for containers) and 17–24 are inert. Bit 15 only becomes observable with a real needing all 17 digits, so `mixed_doc` includes `0.30000000000000004`.\n- Row 49: precision 31 genuinely overflows `MAX_REAL_STR_LENGTH` for some inputs and both libraries return NULL identically.\n\n### Unrelated pre-existing failure\n`cargo test --release` (whole suite) aborts in **`a05_strconv_dtoa`** with SIGSEGV when its tests run in parallel; it passes cleanly with `--test-threads=1`. That is a pre-existing thread-safety issue in `dtoa`'s static freelist exercised by that file — untouched by this work.","usage":{"total_tokens":222190,"tool_uses":69,"duration_ms":1195816},"uuid":"cd2fac6a-e58a-4d87-b0e3-3945c6d34081","session_id":"e1728859-25d0-4463-adf4-96231e8435a5"}
