 and assert they agree on\n  (a) NULL-ness of the returned `json_t*`,\n  (b) the FULL `json_error_t` byte image via `.raw()` — start from `json_error_t::poisoned()` so \"did not write\" is distinguishable from \"wrote NUL\"; this pins line, column, position, source, text and the code byte all at once,\n  (c) the specific `json_error_code()` value — assert it equals the exact numeric code the ERRORS.md row documents, so the test proves \"the same error\", not merely \"both failed\".\nAdditionally cover the generic FFI boundaries for every `json_load*` entry point even where ERRORS.md has no row: NULL input pointer, NULL error pointer (must be tolerated), zero length, oversized/`(size_t)-1` length, an invalid file descriptor, a path that is a directory, and FLAG WORDS WITH UNDEFINED BITS SET (0x20, 0x8000, `SIZE_MAX`, and random 64-bit words) — a C `size_t flags` accepts any value and both implementations must ignore unknown bits identically.\n\nSeveral rows are OOM-only (e.g. 185, 197 and the `strbuffer`/`lex_init` failures). Reach them with a failing or budgeted allocator installed via `json_set_alloc_funcs`/`json_set_alloc_funcs2`, exactly as `a09_errors_lowlevel.rs` does — install it on BOTH libraries with the same budget, and always restore the originals before the test returns. NOTE from ERRORS.md row 197: on the `lex_init` OOM path the error struct is left only partially initialised, so compare what both libraries write but do NOT assert a specific code byte there.\n\nRows that ERRORS.md marks as reaching a live `assert()` (SIGABRT) or a segfault are NOT testable in-process — rows 343, 345, 347 are called out as unreachable/internal invariants. For those, add a clearly-commented `#[test]` that documents WHY it cannot be exercised (quoting the C) rather than a fake passing assertion, and leave the row unchecked in ERRORS.md with a one-line reason appended to the row's `verified` cell (e.g. `[-] unreachable: assert`).\n\nSTEP 3 — run: `cd translation && cargo bui