on the exact observable — the return value (`NULL` for `json_dumps`, `-1` for `json_dumpf`/`json_dumpfd`/`json_dump_file`/`json_dump_callback`, and note from ERRORS.md that **`json_dumpb` returns 0 on error, which is indistinguishable from an empty dump**) plus, where a buffer is involved, the full byte image of that buffer.\n\nRows that need particular care, per ERRORS.md:\n- 200: `json == NULL` without `JSON_ENCODE_ANY` → `-1` before `hashtable_init`.\n- 207: a string containing invalid UTF-8, built via `json_string_nocheck(\"\\xff\")`, makes `utf8_iterate` fail inside `dump_string` → `NULL`.\n- 208/209: `jsonp_dtostr` failing for a real. ERRORS.md records that `JSON_REAL_PRECISION(n)` with n in 22..24 trips the `jsonp_dtostr` length check for `json_real(0.1)`, and n >= 25 trips `dtoa_r`'s short-buffer NULL, because `MAX_REAL_STR_LENGTH` is only 25. Sweep n over 0..=31 for a set of hard doubles and require both libraries to fail at exactly the same (value, precision) pairs.\n- 210: `JSON_SORT_KEYS` with the `key_len` array allocation failing (use a budgeted allocator).\n- 198/202: `strbuffer_init`/`hashtable_init` OOM.\n- 352/353: ERRORS.md notes `dump_string`'s return value is IGNORED at both object-key call sites (sorted and unsorted), so a callback failure on a KEY is silently swallowed while the same failure on a VALUE aborts the dump. Assert that asymmetry explicitly in both libraries.\n- 354: a failing `jsonp_realloc` shrink at the end of `json_dumps` is NOT an error — the larger buffer is returned. Assert success, not failure.\n- 356: circular references. Note the C refuses direct self-insertion (`json == value`), so build INDIRECT cycles (`a=[b]`, `b=[a]`, and a 3-cycle) and also a shared-subtree DAG which must still SUCCEED.\n\nReach the OOM rows with a failing or budgeted allocator installed via `json_set_alloc_funcs`/`json_set_alloc_funcs2` on BOTH libraries with the same budget, exactly as `a09_errors_lowlevel.rs` does; always restore the originals before returning.\n\nAlso cover the generic FFI boundaries even where ERRORS.md has no row: NULL `json`, NULL buffer to `json_dumpb`, size 0, oversized `(size_t)-1` size, a NULL callback pointer to `json_dump_callback`, an invalid/closed/read-only file descriptor, an unopenable path, and FLAG WORDS WITH UNDEFINED BITS SET (0x800, 0x20000, `SIZE_MAX`, random 64-bit words) — `size_t flags` accepts any value and both must ignore unknown bits identically.\n\nIf a row is genuinely unreachable in-process (a live `assert()`/SIGABRT or a segfault), add a clearly-commented `#[test]` documenting WHY, quoting the C, rather than a fake passing assertion — and mark that ERRORS.md row `[-] <reason>` instead of `[x]`.\n\nSTEP 3 — run: `cd translation && cargo build --release && timeout 600 cargo test --release --test a12_errors_dump 2>&1 | tail -40`. Iterate to green.\n\nSTEP 4 — on ANY divergence, fix `translation/src/dump.rs` to match the C exactly, `cargo build --release`, re-run, and ALSO re-run `cargo test --release --test a06_dump` to confirm no regression.\n\nSTEP 5 — in `translation/ERRORS.md`, change the `verified` cell from `[ ]` to `[x]` for each assigned row that now has a passing test (or `[-] <reason>`). Touch ONLY your assigned row numbers.\n\nReport: test count, pass/fail, every Rust bug fixed with file and before/after, every row marked `[-]` and why, and any bug you suspect lies elsewhere."}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3287,"cache_read_input_tokens":320038,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3287,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e1728859-25d0-4463-adf4-96231e8435a5","uuid":"ca044c12-ff22-4ae0-8c29-87c543e55ef1","timestamp":"2026-08-31T04:25:10.364Z"}
