{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_fjs5v7jeae2eaoy5ns7aa5dwr6tyo3ircbuuo7ohl2kbrrxr57aq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Report\n\n**File written:** `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-6oQ0Bh/translation/tests/a11_errors_load.rs` (25 `#[test]` functions, ~2900 lines)\n\n**Result: 25 passed, 0 failed.** `a07_load` re-run: 22 passed, 0 failed (no regression).\n\n### Rust bugs fixed\n**None.** `translation/src/load.rs` needed no changes — every one of my assigned rows agreed with the C on all three observables (NULL-ness, the full 252-byte `json_error_t` image from a `poisoned()` start, and the exact numeric `json_error_code()`). I verified the harness is not vacuous: temporarily flipping one flag bit on the Rust side of `d_loads` made 7 tests fail with real `C/Rust divergence` messages, and it caught 5 wrong *expectations* of my own during development.\n\nThe strongest single result is the **allocation-sequence parity**: with a budgeted `json_set_alloc_funcs2` allocator installed on both libraries, I swept budgets `0..N` for 10 documents through `json_loads`/`json_loadb`/`json_load_callback` and compared the full error image at *every* budget. Both libraries fail at exactly the same allocation index — i.e. the Rust performs the identical malloc/realloc sequence, which is what makes rows 162-164, 169, 172, 175, 185 and 197 reachable at all.\n\n### Rows marked `[-]` in ERRORS.md (with reason appended to the `verified` cell)\n| row | reason |\n|---|---|\n| 343 | `assert(0)` after `utf8_encode`; `value` is range- and surrogate-checked first (SIGABRT, not testable in-process) |\n| 344 | `assert(0)` in the escape `switch` `default:`; the first pass already rejected every illegal escape |\n| 345 | `assert(count >= 2)`; `utf8_check_first` returns `0` (handled by the `goto out` above) or `2..4` for bytes ≥ 0x80 |\n| 347 | `assert(c == d)`; every `unget` is paired with a preceding `get` of the same byte |\n| 348 | `assert(end == saved_text + length)`; the lexer validated the literal char-by-char |\n\nThese are documented in `rows_343_348_live_asserts_are_unreachable_by_construction`, which quotes the relevant C and — instead of a fake passing assertion — proves the *guards* that make each assert unreachable: all 128 escape characters, all 20 `\\uXXXX` boundary codepoints plus 200 random ones, all 128 bytes `0x80..0xFF` through the exported `utf8_check_first`, and the integer-literal/unget round-trips.\n\nRows **145-197, 339, 340, 341, 355** are now `[x]`. Rows 342, 346 and 349 were left untouched (not in my assignment).\n\n### Two ERRORS.md *documentation* inaccuracies found (I did not edit the trigger/expected columns)\n1. **Row 193** claims input `\"[\\\"\\xff\\\"]\"` yields text `\"unable to decode byte 0xff\"`. The real C library yields `\"unable to decode byte 0xff near '\\\"'\"` — the opening `\"` is already in `saved_text`, so `error_set`'s `STREAM_STATE_ERROR` no-context branch is not taken. The bare form occurs only when the bad byte is *token-initial* (`lex_scan` clears `saved_text` first), e.g. `\"\\xff[]\"` or `\"[\\xc0\\x80]\"`. Confirmed with a standalone C program linked against `c_src/build/libjansson.so`. Both forms are now pinned in the test.\n2. **Rows 339/150 interaction:** `json_load_file` sets `error->source` to the path, then delegates to `json_loadf`, whose own `jsonp_error_init(error, \"<stream>\")` **overwrites it**. So the path survives in `source` only on the `fopen`-failure path (row 150). Row 339's `source` is `\"<stream>\"`, not the path.\n\nAlso worth recording: row 191's `\"-99999999999999999999\"` is 21 saved bytes, past `error_set`'s 20-byte context gate, so its text is the bare `\"too big negative integer\"` (the row's quoted `near '...'` form applies to the 20-byte `-9223372036854775809` case).\n\n### Suspected bugs in files I was told not to edit\n**None.** `value.rs` and `pack_unpack.rs` behaved identically to the C on every path these tests reach, including all the container-construction OOM paths driven through the budgeted allocator.\n\n### Extra coverage beyond the assigned rows (generic FFI boundaries)\n- NULL input pointer for all six entry points; NULL `json_error_t*` tolerated on all six, on both the guard paths and the success path (`parse_json` writes `error->position`).\n- `json_loadb` with `buflen == 0`, `SIZE_MAX`, `SIZE_MAX-1`, `SIZE_MAX/2`, `1<<40` (using a NUL-padded 4 KiB buffer so the parser provably stops inside the allocation), and every prefix length of a 29-byte document.\n- Invalid descriptors: negative, closed, never-opened (9997-9999); `json_load_file` on directories (`fopen` succeeds, the read fails with `EISDIR` → code 6, *not* `cannot_open_file`).\n- `stdin` identity: `json_loadf(stdin)` → `\"<stdin>\"` and `json_loadfd(0/1/2)` → `\"<stdin>\"/\"<stream>\"/\"<stream>\"`, driven at allocation budget 0 so `lex_init` fails before any blocking read.\n- Flag words with undefined bits: `0x20`, `0x40`, `0x80`, `0x100`, `0x200`, `0x400`, `0x8000`, `0x10000`, `SIZE_MAX`, `JSON_REAL_PRECISION(17)`, `JSON_INDENT(0/4/31)` and 48 random 64-bit words, asserted byte-identical to `flags & 0x1F` across 13 inputs and all six entry points. (Note `JSON_INDENT(n)` *aliases* the decoder bits rather than being ignored — the comparison is against the masked word, which captures that faithfully.)\n- A 1400-iteration seeded fuzz of documents assembled from 45 error-triggering fragments × randomized flag words × random truncation points, comparing the full error image every time."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":463,"cache_read_input_tokens":205970,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":463,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01RXgwoiuECjpqeirpk1kSob","session_id":"e1728859-25d0-4463-