rm `json_delete` on them, and incref/decref of them, behaves identically and never frees.\n- **Self-insertion.** `json_array_append_new`/`json_object_set*` refuse `json == value`; assert the exact return and that the value is decref'd the same way.\n- **Invalid UTF-8** into the checking variants (`json_string`, `json_stringn`, `json_string_set`, `json_string_setn`, `json_object_set_new`, `json_object_setn_new`) versus the `_nocheck` variants which must ACCEPT it — use the lone-continuation, truncated, overlong, surrogate and 5-byte forms.\n- **Embedded NUL in keys** — note the `n`-suffixed and `nocheck` variants differ from the plain ones here.\n- **Rows 122-125** (`json_deep_copy` / `jsonp_loop_check`): the C rejects direct self-insertion, so build INDIRECT cycles (`a=[b]`, `b=[a]`, plus a 3-cycle and an object↔array cycle), and also a shared-subtree DAG which must still SUCCEED.\n- **Rows 336/337** (`json_object_seed`): the generated-seed-is-zero forcing and the `/dev/urandom` fallback. If these are not reachable in-process (the seed is one-shot and already installed by `both()`), say so and mark them `[-] <reason>` rather than faking a pass.\n\nThe remaining OOM-only rows are reachable with a failing or budgeted allocator installed via `json_set_alloc_funcs`/`json_set_alloc_funcs2` on BOTH libraries with the same budget, exactly as `a09_errors_lowlevel.rs` does. Sweeping the budget and requiring both libraries to fail at the SAME allocation index is a strong check — do that where an operation makes several allocations. Always restore the original allocators before the test returns.\n\nIf a row is genuinely unreachable in-process (a live `assert()`/SIGABRT, a segfault, or dead code), add a clearly-commented `#[test]` documenting WHY, quoting the C, and verifying through the FFI the guard that makes it unreachable — then mark that ERRORS.md row `[-] <reason>` instead of `[x]`. Do not fake a passing assertion.\n\nSTEP 3 — run: `cd translation && cargo build --releas