{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_y54ygsaz5hqgn4kesbq5rwtnonl2enuufbcuu6jr3wyuy7n4iakq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_013ioknR3uh5BBsa6ebVj2DJ","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Phase C error tests for pack_unpack","subagent_type":"general-purpose","prompt":"Working dir: /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-6oQ0Bh\n\nYou are writing PHASE C error-path DIFFERENTIAL tests comparing the C jansson library against its Rust translation across the FFI boundary. The C in `c_src/` is ALWAYS correct ground truth. NEVER modify anything in `c_src/`. On any divergence you MUST fix the RUST source — never change the C and never weaken a test to match the Rust.\n\nAnother agent is concurrently editing `translation/src/value.rs` and `translation/tests/a13_errors_value.rs`. Confine your source fixes to `translation/src/pack_unpack.rs` and `translation/src/varargs.rs`, and do not touch a13. If you conclude a bug is in value.rs, report it in detail instead of editing.\n\nSTEP 1 — read first:\n- `translation/tests/HARNESS.md` — the harness contract. Two MANDATORY rules: every `#[test]` must begin with `let _g = global_state_lock();`, and because of the `.so` staleness guard you must `cargo build --release` after any `src/` change.\n- `translation/tests/a08_pack_unpack.rs` — the existing Phase B pack/unpack tests. Do NOT duplicate them; this is the ERROR-path complement. It already contains excellent machinery you should REUSE: a `Slots` poisoned-out-pointer block, a thread-local allocation-failure hook for reaching the `<internal>` / \"Out of memory\" paths, and a hand-built x86-64 SysV `va_list` that lets you make variadic calls of run-time arity. Read it carefully before writing anything.\n- `translation/tests/a11_errors_load.rs` and `translation/tests/a12_errors_dump.rs` — worked Phase C examples.\n- `c_src/src/pack_unpack.c` in full.\n- `translation/ERRORS.md` — your assigned rows are **87, 88, 221-280** (every row in that set whose `verified` cell is still `[ ]`). Read each row's `trigger` and `expected C result`; they were validated against the real C library.\n\nSTEP 2 — write `translation/tests/a14_errors_pack_unpack.rs` covering EVERY one of those rows.\nFor each row: construct that exact invalid input/condition, call BOTH libraries, and assert they agree on\n  (a) the return value (`NULL` for pack/sprintf, `-1` for unpack),\n  (b) the FULL `json_error_t` byte image via `.raw()`, starting from `json_error_t::poisoned()` so \"did not write\" is distinguishable from \"wrote NUL\" — this pins line, column, position, `source` and `text` and the code byte all at once,\n  (c) the exact numeric `json_error_code()` the row documents, so the test proves \"the same error\", not merely \"both failed\",\n  (d) for unpack, that the poisoned out-pointer block is written (or left untouched) identically.\n\nBehaviours from ERRORS.md that need particular care:\n- **Error stickiness.** `jsonp_error_vset` silently drops any SECOND error, so e.g. `json_pack(\"{s:s}\", \"k\", NULL)` reports `\"NULL string\"` — NOT `\"NULL object value\"`. A port that overwrites would still \"fail\", so assert the exact text/code, not just failure.\n- **Rows 229 and the `*` modifier.** An object value packing to NULL is an error unless the value token is `'*'`.\n- **Rows that record NO error being set.** ERRORS.md notes `json_pack(\"s*\", NULL)` and `json_pack(\"o*\", NULL)` return `NULL` with no error recorded, so `json_error_code()` reads uninitialised memory. For those, compare the return value and whatever both libraries wrote, but do NOT assert a specific code byte — and say so in a comment.\n- **Rows 239, 242, 265, 274** are OOM/`strbuffer` failures reached with the allocation-failure hook (`<internal>` source, \"Out of memory\" text). Row 274 is subtler: the strict-object key-list allocation failing yields `\"<n> object item(s) left unpacked: <unknown>\"`.\n- **Format-string errors**: unexpected/unrecognised format characters in every position (key, value, array element, top level), unterminated `{`/`[`, `\"Expected format 's', got ...\"`, garbage after the format string, tokens after `!`/`*`, and `!`/`*` misplacement.\n- **Type mismatches** for every unpack conversion char against all 8 json types, plus a NULL root and a root with an out-of-range type tag.\n- **Index/key errors**: `json_error_index_out_of_range` for short arrays, `json_error_item_not_found` for missing object keys (and the interaction with `?`).\n\nAlso cover the generic FFI boundaries even where ERRORS.md has no row: NULL format string, empty format string, NULL root, NULL `json_error_t*` (must be tolerated on every entry point), and FLAG WORDS WITH UNDEFINED BITS SET (0x4, 0x8, 0x10, `SIZE_MAX`, and random 64-bit words) — `size_t flags` accepts any value and both implementations must ignore unknown bits identically.\n\nCover the `v*` entry points too — `json_vpack_ex`, `json_vunpack_ex`, `json_vsprintf` — on their ERROR paths specifically, via the `vashim()` trampolines. The C does `va_copy`, so the early-return error path is exactly where vararg handling bugs hide.\n\nIf a row is genuinely unreachable in-process (a live `assert()`/SIGABRT, a segfault, or dead code), add a clearly-commented `#[test]` documenting WHY, quoting the C, and verifying through the FFI the guard that makes it unreachable — then mark that ERRORS.md row `[-] <reason>` instead of `[x]`. Do not fake a passing assertion.\n\nSTEP 3 — run: `cd translation && cargo build --release && timeout 600 cargo test --release --test a14_errors_pack_unpack 2>&1 | tail -40`. Iterate to green.\n\nSTEP 4 — on ANY divergence, fix `translation/src/pack_unpack.rs` (or `varargs.rs`) to match the C exactly, `cargo build --release`, re-run, and ALSO re-run `cargo test --release --test a08_pack_unpack` to confirm no regression.\n\nSTEP 5 — in `translation/ERRORS.md`, change the `verified` cell from `[ ]` to `[x]` for each assigned row that now passes (or `[-] <reason>`). Touch ONLY your assigned row numbers (87, 88, 221-280).\n\nReport: test count, pass/fail, every Rust bug fixed with file and before/after, every row marked `[-]` and why, any bug you suspect lies elsewhere, and how you validated the tests are not vacuous (e.g. by temporarily injecting a bug into pack_unpack.rs, confirming failures, then reverting)."}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":599,"cache_read_input_tokens":351846,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":599,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e1728859-25d0-4463-adf4-96231e8435a5","uuid":"0264fb88-51e2-4676-a771-484bf1b38b2e","timestamp":"2026-08-31T04:54:55.671Z"}
