{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_015FePHvFF4QrV2a342Fuesw","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Differential test harness contract\n2\t\n3\tEvery test file in `translation/tests/` is a standalone integration test that\n4\tloads BOTH shared objects via `libloading` and compares their behaviour. No test\n5\tever calls a Rust function directly — everything goes through the `.so` exports.\n6\t\n7\t## Boilerplate\n8\t\n9\t```rust\n10\tmod common;\n11\tuse common::*;\n12\tuse std::ffi::{c_char, c_int, c_void};\n13\t\n14\t#[test]\n15\tfn my_test() {\n16\t    let (c, r) = both();   // (&'static Api for C, &'static Api for Rust)\n17\t    unsafe {\n18\t        // ... call (c.json_foo)(..) and (r.json_foo)(..) and compare\n19\t    }\n20\t}\n21\t```\n22\t\n23\t`both()` returns the two `Api` structs and guarantees `json_object_seed(FIXED_SEED)`\n24\thas already been called on BOTH libraries. That matters: jansson seeds its hash\n25\tfrom `/dev/urandom` on first use, and the seed decides object iteration order and\n26\ttherefore the exact bytes of every object dump. Always use `both()`, never\n27\t`capi()`/`rapi()` directly.\n28\t\n29\t## `Api`\n30\t\n31\t`Api` has one field per exported symbol, named exactly like the C symbol, typed\n32\tas an `unsafe extern \"C\" fn` pointer. Call them as `(c.json_loads)(...)`.\n33\t\n34\tTwo symbols are DATA, not functions, and have accessor methods instead:\n35\t- `c.dtoa_divmax() -> c_int`\n36\t- `c.hashtable_seed() -> u32`\n37\t\n38\t## Comparing\n39\t\n40\tUse the `diff_eq!` macro — it prints which configuration diverged:\n41\t\n42\t```rust\n43\tdiff_eq!(c_value, rust_value, \"json_dumps(flags={flags:#x}) on {input:?}\");\n44\t```\n45\t\n46\tThe context string is a `format!` pattern; always include enough to reproduce\n47\tthe case (flags, the input, the loop index).\n48\t\n49\t## Helpers in `common`\n50\t\n51\t| helper | purpose |\n52\t|---|---|\n53\t| `cs(\"text\") -> CString` | NUL-terminated C string; panics on interior NUL |\n54\t| `cs_bytes(b\"a\\0b\") -> Vec<c_char>` | NUL-terminated buffer that MAY contain interior NULs |\n55\t| `cbytes(ptr) -> Option<Vec<u8>>` | read a `char*` as raw bytes; `None` for NULL, so \"returned NULL\" stays distinct from \"returned empty string\" |\n56\t| `cstr_lossy(ptr) -> String` | for messages only |\n57\t| `incref(j)` / `decref(api, j)` | `json_incref`/`json_decref` are `static inline` in jansson.h so they are NOT exported; these reproduce them exactly. `decref` must be given the SAME api the value came from. |\n58\t| `typeof_(j) -> c_int` | `json_typeof` |\n59\t| `jfree(api, ptr)` | free a `char*` jansson allocated, via that library's allocator |\n60\t| `Api::hashtable_seed()`, `Api::dtoa_divmax()` | the two data symbols |\n61\t\n62\tNever free a C-library pointer with the Rust library's allocator or vice versa.\n63\t\n64\t## Types available\n65\t\n66\t`json_t` (`{ type_: c_int, refcount: size_t }`), `json_error_t`, `hashtable_t`,\n67\t`strbuffer_t`, `FILE`, `json_int_t` (= i64), `size_t`.\n68\t\n69\t`json_error_t::new()` zeroes it; `json_error_t::poisoned()` fills it with a\n70\tsentinel so \"the library did not write here\" is distinguishable from \"it wrote a\n71\tNUL\". Compare errors with `.raw()` (full byte image — strongest), or\n72\t`.snapshot()` / `.code()` / `.text_str()` for readable assertions.\n73\t\n74\tAll flag constants are defined: `JSON_COMPACT`, `JSON_ENSURE_ASCII`,\n75\t`JSON_SORT_KEYS`, `JSON_PRESERVE_ORDER`, `JSON_ENCODE_ANY`, `JSON_ESCAPE_SLASH`,\n76\t`JSON_EMBED`, `JSON_REJECT_DUPLICATES`, `JSON_DISABLE_EOF_CHECK`,\n77\t`JSON_DECODE_ANY`, `JSON_DECODE_INT_AS_REAL`, `JSON_ALLOW_NUL`,\n78\t`JSON_VALIDATE_ONLY`, `JSON_STRICT`, plus the helpers `json_indent(n)` and\n79\t`json_real_precision(n)`, the `JSON_*` type tags, the `JSON_ERROR_*` code\n80\tconstants, and `JSON_PARSER_MAX_DEPTH`.\n81\t\n82\t## Randomised (property-style) testing\n83\t\n84\tUse the seeded `Rng` (SplitMix64) so every run is reproducible. Give each test\n85\tits own fixed seed constant.\n86\t\n87\t```rust\n88\tlet mut rng = Rng::new(0x1234_5678);\n89\trng.next_u64(); rng.next_u32(); rng.below(n); rng.range(lo, hi); rng.bool();\n90\trng.choice(&slice);\n91\trng.json_int();          // biased to 0, ±1, INT32/INT64 bounds\n92\trng.real();              // biased to 0.0, -0.0, subnormals, 1e±308, 17-digit values\n93\trng.ascii_string(max);   // pool includes quotes, backslash, slash, control chars\n94\trng.utf8_string(max);    // spans 1-,2-,3-,4-byte sequences + control chars\n95\t```\n96\t\n97\tPrefer MANY randomised inputs per configuration over one hand-picked value, and\n98\tcompare after EVERY step of a mutation sequence rather than only at the end.\n99\t\n100\t## va_list entry points\n101\t\n102\t`json_vpack_ex`, `json_vunpack_ex`, `json_vsprintf` and `jsonp_error_vset` take a\n103\t`va_list`, which stable Rust cannot construct. `vashim()` returns trampolines\n104\t(compiled from `tests/vashim.c`) that are variadic on the Rust side and forward a\n105\treal `va_list`. Get the target function's address with\n106\t`sym_addr(\"C\", b\"json_vpack_ex\")` / `sym_addr(\"Rust\", b\"json_vpack_ex\")`:\n107\t\n108\t```rust\n109\tlet sh = vashim();\n110\tlet cfn = sym_addr(\"C\", b\"json_vpack_ex\");\n111\tlet rfn = sym_addr(\"Rust\", b\"json_vpack_ex\");\n112\tlet cj = (sh.vpack_ex)(cfn, &mut cerr, flags, fmt.as_ptr(), /* varargs... */);\n113\tlet rj = (sh.vpack_ex)(rfn, &mut rerr, flags, fmt.as_ptr(), /* varargs... */);\n114\t```\n115\t\n116\tShim signatures (first arg is always the target function pointer):\n117\t- `sh.vpack_ex(fn, *mut json_error_t, size_t flags, *const c_char fmt, ...) -> *mut json_t`\n118\t- `sh.vunpack_ex(fn, *mut json_t root, *mut json_error_t, size_t flags, *const c_char fmt, ...) -> c_int`\n119\t- `sh.vsprintf(fn, *const c_char fmt, ...) -> *mut json_t`\n120\t- `sh.error_vset(fn, *mut json_error_t, c_int line, c_int col, size_t pos, c_int code, *const c_char msg, ...)`\n121\t\n122\tWhen passing varargs, give ints an explicit type (`7 as c_int`), doubles as\n123\t`f64`, and strings as `.as_ptr()` of a `CString` kept alive in a local.\n124\t\n125\t## Running\n126\t\n127\t```\n128\tcd translation && cargo test --release --test <name>\n129\t```\n130\t`--release` is required: the tests dlopen `target/release/libjansson.so`.\n131\tCargo is configured offline (`translation/.cargo/config.toml`); crates.io is\n132\tunreachable in this environment.\n133\t\n134\t`../run_tests.sh` rebuilds the C `.so`, the Rust `.so` and the va shim, checks\n135\t`nm -D` symbol parity, then runs the whole suite.\n136\t\n137\t## MANDATORY: every test must take `global_state_lock()`\n138\t\n139\t```rust\n140\t#[test]\n141\tfn my_test() {\n142\t    let _g = global_state_lock();     // FIRST statement, always\n143\t    let (c, r) = both();\n144\t    ...\n145\t}\n146\t```\n147\t\n148\tCargo runs a binary's `#[test]` functions on several threads, but two pieces of\n149\tthe C library's state are process-global and **not** thread-safe:\n150\t\n151\t1. **dtoa's `Balloc`/`Bfree` freelist.** `dtoa.c` is compiled without\n152\t   `MULTIPLE_THREADS` defined, so `freelist[]` is a bare global with no locking.\n153\t   Any two threads formatting a real concurrently — which includes any\n154\t   `json_dumps` of a document containing a real — corrupt it. The symptom is\n155\t   nasty: the **C** side starts returning plausible-looking but wrong digits\n156\t   (e.g. `904692889061487.1882` dumping as `200000000000000.0`), so it reads as a\n157\t   translation bug when it is really a harness race.\n158\t2. **The allocator function pointers** in memory.c, which\n159\t   `json_set_alloc_funcs*` mutates.\n160\t\n161\tHolding the lock for the whole test body makes each file serialise internally,\n162\tso results do not depend on `--test-threads`. Separate test binaries are separate\n163\tprocesses with their own `dlopen`ed copies, so they do not interfere.\n164\t\n165\t## The `.so` staleness guard\n166\t\n167\t`cargo test --test <name>` does **not** rebuild `libjansson.so`: the integration\n168\ttest `dlopen`s it rather than linking it, so cargo sees no dependency. The\n169\tharness therefore refuses to run if any `src/*.rs` is newer than the `.so`.\n170\tAlways `cargo build --release` first, or just use `../run_tests.sh`.\n171\t\n172\t## Rules\n173\t\n174\t- The C is ALWAYS right. On divergence, fix the Rust in `translation/src/`, never\n175\t  the C and never the test's expectation.\n176\t- Never modify anything in `c_src/`.\n177\t- Compare raw bytes (`cbytes`, `.raw()`), not lossy strings, for anything the C\n178\t  produces — jansson can emit non-UTF-8 with the `_nocheck` entry points.\n179\t- Free what you allocate, with the matching library's allocator, so a long\n180\t  randomised loop does not exhaust memory.\n181\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_013ioknR3uh5BBsa6ebVj2DJ","session_id":"e1728859-25d0-4463-adf4-96231e8435a5","uuid":"a294c46d-05e7-4900-b324-a1589c3de1fb","timestamp":"2026-08-31T04:54:58.833Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Phase C error tests for pack_unpack"}
