{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_qt2hlo675ctcqedphntn2lyqcqtweatjt73b2glbzreb6243inua","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. Final report:\n\n## What was done\n\nWrote `translation/tests/a13_errors_value.rs` (3225 lines, **24 `#[test]`s, all passing**) — the Phase C error-path differential complement to `a04_value.rs`. Ran green alongside every other suite (a00–a07, a09–a12; a08 left alone as instructed).\n\n```\ncargo build --release && cargo test --release --test a13_errors_value\ntest result: ok. 24 passed; 0 failed\n```\n\n**Coverage:** every assigned ERRORS.md row — 1-11, 13-57, 59-71, 74-82, 84-86, 91-94, 96-100, 105-120, 122-125, 336, 337.\n\nKey techniques used, beyond \"both returned -1\":\n- **Table-driven wrong-type sweep.** An 18-entry \"zoo\" (all 8 real types + 2 extra shapes, 7 out-of-range type tags, NULL) built in *both* libraries, crossed against every `json_is_*`-guarded accessor and mutator, plus an 18×18 sweep of the `json_object_update*` family and `json_array_extend`. Each entry's full state is snapshotted before and after to prove a rejected call changes nothing.\n- **Refcount witness.** Values handed to failing mutators are increfd to 2 first, so \"decref'd exactly once\" is observable as `refcount == 1` on both sides (catches both a missing and a double decref) without a use-after-free.\n- **Out-of-range type tags across the FFI.** Stack `json_t { type_: -1/8/9/99/256/i32::MAX/i32::MIN, refcount: usize::MAX }` pseudo-singletons, passed to `json_equal`/`json_copy`/`json_deep_copy`/`json_dumps`/`json_delete`/every typed accessor, and *embedded in real containers* (dump fails, deep copy fails, shallow copy succeeds, `update_recursive` treats it as a leaf). `256` is deliberate: its low byte is `JSON_OBJECT`, so a byte-truncating port would crash.\n- **Indirect cycles for rows 122-125**: `a=[b],b=[a]`; `a={b:b},b={a:a}`; a 3-cycle; an array↔object cycle — each entered from every node, twice, with whole-graph refcounts compared — plus a shared-subtree DAG negative control that must still *succeed* and must not share children.\n- **Budgeted allocator with per-library counters.** Rows 32/63/69/118/119/125. For `json_copy`/`json_deep_copy`/`json_object_update_recursive` the budget is swept `0..=total`, asserting both libraries fail at the **same allocation index** *and* consume the **same number of allocations**. Allocators are restored by a `Drop` guard so a panic can't poison later tests.\n- Rows 336/337 needed a **subprocess**: `json_object_seed` is one-shot and `both()` has already seeded, and C's `seed_initialized` is a file-local static. The test re-execs itself with `--exact`, drops `RLIMIT_NOFILE` to 3 so `open(\"/dev/urandom\")` must fail, brackets the seeding with `gettimeofday`, and asserts each library's seed equals `((sec ^ usec) ^ pid)` for a timestamp inside the bracket. Verified non-vacuous: `/dev/urandom` *is* readable here, and the observed seeds (`0x6a98a7af` / `0x6a98a7a8`, pid 7) differ by exactly the microseconds between the two calls — both took the fallback.\n\n## Rust bugs fixed\n\n**None.** `translation/src/value.rs` matched the C on every one of these error paths; no source change was needed, so nothing in `src/` was modified (verified byte-identical to its starting state).\n\n## Rows marked `[-]`\n\n- **336** (`generate_seed()` computes 0 → forced to 1): not forceable. Its inputs are 4 bytes of `/dev/urandom` or `gettimeofday() ^ getpid()`; none can be steered so the XOR is 0. The reason marked `[-]` rather than faked: the only observable consequence — the installed seed is never 0 — *is* asserted in the subprocess test. Row 337 is marked `[x]` (the `open() == -1` branch is genuinely exercised in both libraries; the \"short read\" half is not forceable but shares the return path).\n\nAll other 112 assigned rows are now `[x]` in `translation/ERRORS.md`; a normalized diff confirms nothing but those checkbox cells (and row 336's reason text) changed.\n\n## Non-vacuity validation\n\nInjected 8 bugs into `value.rs` one at a time (backup/restore, rebuild each time); **all 8 were caught**, each by the test that targets it:\n\n| mutation | result |\n|---|---|\n| drop `json == value` in `json_object_setn_new_nocheck` | `self_insertion_rejected_identically`, `update_recursive_cycle_and_inner_failure` FAILED |\n| drop `is_infinite()` in `json_real_set` | `real_rejects_nan_and_inf` FAILED |\n| `index > entries` → `>=` in `json_array_insert_new` | `array_index_out_of_range` FAILED |\n| remove `json_decref(value)` on the NULL-key path | `null_key_arguments_are_rejected_and_the_value_is_decrefd` FAILED |\n| `json_copy` `default:` returns `json` not NULL | `out_of_range_type_tags_take_the_same_default_arm` FAILED |\n| `jsonp_loop_check` stops reporting duplicates | `deep_copy_rejects_indirect_cycles` → stack overflow / SIGABRT |\n| `json_string_length` loses its type guard | 17 of 24 tests FAILED |\n| `json_equal` NULL check `||` → `&&` | SIGSEGV in `json_equal_rejections` |\n\n`value.rs` was then restored (`diff` clean) and a04/a06/a07/a03/a01 re-run: no regressions.\n\n## Things worth flagging elsewhere\n\n- **ERRORS.md row 121 is still `[ ]`**, although the task brief listed it as already `[x]`. It *is* now proven — the `budget == 0` step of my deep-copy sweep is exactly \"`hashtable_init(&parents_set)` fails → NULL\" — but it is outside my assigned row list so I left the cell alone. Whoever owns it can flip it; the note in a13's header records this. The same applies to row 83, which `strndup_length_overflow_is_rejected` proves without an allocator hook (the `len + 1` wrap to 0 makes `jsonp_malloc(0)` return NULL). That row was already `[x]`.\n- **Two inputs I deliberately did not test**, because they are memory-unsafe in the *C* too (so a differential test would only prove both crash): a large `key_len` to `json_object_setn_*`/`getn`/`deln` (ERRORS.md's row-297 note — `hashlittle` 